Fix an infinite loop in iconv() with sub = "Unicode" or sub = "c99" on
input that is not valid UTF-8.

    iconv("a\xc3(", "UTF-8", "ASCII", sub = "Unicode")

never returns.  When the converter stops on an invalid or truncated
sequence, the "Unicode" and "c99" substitution branches of do_iconv()
decode the offending bytes with utf8toucs() to name the code point.
If that decoding fails (clen <= 0, or fewer bytes left than it needs)
nothing is written and, crucially, nothing is consumed; the code then
jumps back to Riconv(), which fails at the very same byte, and so on
forever.  The sub = "byte" branch always advances by at least one byte,
which is why it does not loop.

These two modes are the documented way to make untrusted text printable
(?iconv recommends them over sub = "byte" for UTF-8 input), so a single
stray byte in a file or web response hangs the session.  Reproduces in
released R 4.6.1.

When there is no code point to name, substitute the byte exactly as
sub = "byte" does ("<c3>") and consume it, so the loop makes progress.
The surrounding buffer checks already guarantee room for 13 (Unicode)
or 11 (c99) bytes, more than the 4 this writes.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (as a timeout).

Upstream status: NOT YET REPORTED to the R Bugzilla as of 2026-10-08.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/main/sysutils.c
+++ b/src/main/sysutils.c
@@ -834,6 +834,15 @@
 			    size_t l = strlen(outbuf);
 			    outbuf += l; outb -= l;
 			}
+		    } else {
+			/* Not a UTF-8 sequence at all (or cut short):
+			   there is no code point to name, so substitute
+			   the byte as sub = "byte" would.  Something must
+			   be consumed, or the next Riconv() call fails at
+			   the same byte forever. */
+			snprintf(outbuf, 5, "<%02x>", (unsigned char)*inbuf);
+			outbuf += 4; outb -= 4;
+			inbuf++; inb--;
 		    }
 		    goto next_char;
 		} else if(fromUTF8 && streql(sub, "c99")) {
@@ -862,6 +871,11 @@
 			    snprintf(outbuf, 11, "\\U%08x", (unsigned int) ucs);
 			    outbuf += 10; outb -= 10;
 			}
+		    } else {
+			/* As above: an invalid byte has no code point. */
+			snprintf(outbuf, 5, "<%02x>", (unsigned char)*inbuf);
+			outbuf += 4; outb -= 4;
+			inbuf++; inb--;
 		    }
 		    goto next_char;
 		} else {
